# Vagrant Up Hang on Windows — Troubleshooting

2026-09-29

## Problem

`vagrant up` in `C:\DevOps\BashScripts` (Git Bash on Windows, VirtualBox provider) would not start the `scriptbox` VM. The symptoms got worse as we retried:

- `vagrant up` printed nothing, then sat with a blank cursor.
- `vagrant status` also hung, even though `vagrant --version` answered instantly (Vagrant 2.4.9).
- Later runs froze at the same line every time: `scriptbox: Setting the name of the VM: BashScripts_scriptbox_...`
- New commands failed with a lock error: *"An action 'up' was attempted on the machine 'scriptbox', but another process is already executing an action on the machine."*
- `vagrant ssh scriptbox` failed with *"VM must be running to open SSH connection."*

## Root cause

VirtualBox was overloaded and holding stale state from earlier sessions, so Vagrant kept waiting on it. Four things stacked up:

1. **A stuck VM.** An old `BashScripts_scriptbox_1790617170679_95804` was frozen in the **Stopping** state, which kept VirtualBox busy.
2. **Five forgotten VMs.** The `Manual_provisioning_WinMacIntel` VMs (db01, mc01, rmq01, app01, web01) were still running in the background, using memory and CPU.
3. **Leftover Vagrant processes.** Pressing `Ctrl+C` did not fully stop earlier runs. Two `ruby.exe` processes kept a lock on `scriptbox`, causing the "another process is already executing" error.
4. **18 `VBoxHeadless.exe` processes** in Task Manager, the running VMs plus the stuck one.

Vagrant and VirtualBox themselves were healthy: `vagrant --version` and `VBoxManage list vms` both answered immediately.

## Solution

Clear out every stuck process and VM, then start one VM on a clean slate:

1. Kill leftover Vagrant processes: `taskkill //F //IM ruby.exe`
2. Close VirtualBox Manager, then force-stop all VirtualBox processes (or restart Windows):

```bash
taskkill //F //IM VBoxHeadless.exe
taskkill //F //IM VBoxNetDHCP.exe
taskkill //F //IM VBoxSVC.exe
```

3. Reopen VirtualBox and delete both broken `BashScripts_scriptbox...` VMs (right-click → **Remove → Delete all files**).
4. Start only the VM you need, without clicking in the terminal while it runs:

```bash
cd /c/DevOps/BashScripts
vagrant up scriptbox
```

## Every step we took

| # | What we did | Command or action | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Started the VM | `vagrant up` | No output, blank cursor |
| 2 | Checked the project state | `vagrant status` | Also hung |
| 3 | Checked Vagrant itself | `vagrant --version` | `Vagrant 2.4.9` instantly, so Vagrant was fine |
| 4 | Tested VirtualBox directly | `"/c/Program Files/Oracle/VirtualBox/VBoxManage.exe" list vms` | Listed 9 VMs, so VirtualBox responded |
| 5 | Checked which VMs were running | `VBoxManage.exe list runningvms` | 5 Manual_provisioning VMs running in the background |
| 6 | Tried shutting those down and retried | `vagrant halt` in their folder / `vagrant status` | `vagrant status` hung again |
| 7 | Retried `vagrant up` | `vagrant up` | Got further: status listed 4 VMs, then froze at "Setting the name of the VM" |
| 8 | Tried to destroy and start one VM | `vagrant destroy -f`, `vagrant up scriptbox` | Lock error: another process already executing an action |
| 9 | Killed leftover Vagrant processes | `taskkill //F //IM ruby.exe` | Killed 2 `ruby.exe` processes; `vagrant.exe` not found |
| 10 | Destroyed and restarted scriptbox | `vagrant destroy -f`, `vagrant up scriptbox` | Destroy worked; up froze again at "Setting the name of the VM" |
| 11 | Opened VirtualBox Manager | Visual check | Old scriptbox stuck in **Stopping**; 5 Manual_provisioning VMs **Running**; new scriptbox Powered Off |
| 12 | Checked Task Manager (Details, filter `vbox`) | Visual check | 18 `VBoxHeadless.exe`, 3 `VBoxNetDHCP.exe`, `VBoxSVC.exe` running |
| 13 | Force-stopped VirtualBox processes, deleted broken scriptbox VMs, ran `vagrant up scriptbox` | See Solution | **Fixed** |

**Along the way:** a grey highlighted block kept appearing in the terminal. That is a text selection. In the Windows console, a selection can pause a running program, so avoid clicking inside the window while Vagrant runs.

## Prevention and quick reference

- Run `vagrant halt` in a project's folder when you finish with it, so VMs don't pile up in the background.
- Check what's running before starting new VMs: `VBoxManage.exe list runningvms`.
- Let `vagrant up` finish; don't press `Ctrl+C` mid-run or start a second `vagrant` command in parallel.
- Don't click inside the terminal while Vagrant runs.
- Watch for a hidden Windows permission prompt (`Alt+Tab`) when VirtualBox sets up networking.
- If a Vagrantfile has several VMs, start one at a time: `vagrant up scriptbox`.

| Task | Command (Git Bash) |
| --- | --- |
| List all VMs | `"/c/Program Files/Oracle/VirtualBox/VBoxManage.exe" list vms` |
| List running VMs | `"/c/Program Files/Oracle/VirtualBox/VBoxManage.exe" list runningvms` |
| Kill stuck Vagrant | `taskkill //F //IM ruby.exe` |
| Kill stuck VMs | `taskkill //F //IM VBoxHeadless.exe` |
| Stop VMs cleanly | `vagrant halt` |
| Delete a project's VMs | `vagrant destroy -f` |
| Debug a hang | `vagrant up scriptbox --debug > vagrant_debug.log 2>&1` |
